# Scripts for generating reference file data

Here, we have provided some example scripts that can set up the reference files and potentially useful data file compatible with DANSy analysis. Their uses are as follows:

## Generating a whole human proteome reference file

The [whole_proteome_reference](scripts/whole_proteome_reference.py) script contains all the code to create a single reference file (.csv file) generated by CoDIAC that DANSy uses to extract domain n-grams. The reference file will be added to the DANSY_DATA folder. In its base state the script relies on the GENCODE SwissProt annotations that can be retrieved from their [Metadata files](https://www.gencodegenes.org/human/). Currently, the script looks for the release version 47, which can be directly downloaded from [their FTP site](https://ftp.ebi.ac.uk/pub/databases/gencode/Gencode_human/release_47/gencode.v47.metadata.SwissProt.gz).

You can run the code in the command line if you activate an environment with CoDIAC installed (such as the codiac-env provided in the provided [codiac.yml](codiac.yml) file). Assuming the environment is called codiac-env run the following lines and the reference file will be added.

    conda activate codiac-env
    python scripts/whole_proteome_reference.py

### Modifying the reference file script

By default, the script uses the GENCODE file but can use pybiomart to get UniProt IDs. All that needs to be changed is the **method** variable by setting it to:
    
    method = 'pybiomart'

Additionally, we have currently set it so that CoDIAC generates only a single file, but if you have issues retrieving information from UniProt (should not happen), try setting the single_file to False as it will separate the API requests into smaller chunks. We have ensured DANSy works with multiple reference files for these cases.

If you want to change the directory that the data will be stored or the actual name of the file, the data_folder and file_suffix variables control that.

## Generating Intermediate Domain n-gram Adjacency Files

If using DANSy to extract domain n-grams from the whole proteome, it can be useful to sometimes have the adjacency matrix available outside of the DANSy object to transfer between scripts. In the [complete_proteome_adjacency](scripts/complete_proteome_adjacency.py) script, we provide several functions that help in creating and importing these intermediate files.

### Files generated

If you run the following code in an environment with dansy installed (in this example called dansy)

    conda activate dansy
    python scripts/complete_proteome_adjacency.py

This will create 2 files:

- DANSY_Complete_Proteome_Adjacency.json
- DANSY_Complete_Proteome_Collapsed_ngrams.csv

Within the script, we also provide import functions for these including the `import_proteome_data()` function that imports both data files, the `import_adjacency()` for only the adjacency file, and the `import_collapsed_ngrams()` for the collapsed n-grams only.